  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 320,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "﻿Companies everywhere are forcing AI down the \nthroats of consumers and workers. The tech sector  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5520,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has reached peak absurdity: a billionaire \nwho has never worked a 9-to-5 went from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9920,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "predicting civilization collapse to claiming \nChatGPT is essential for raising children;  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14720,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a chipmaker proclaims that virtual AI agents \nwill replace teachers, nurses, and doctors;  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19920,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Google’s CEO declares AI an even more profound \ndiscovery than fire or electricity. On the other  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26400,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "side of the world, a Japanese mogul who lost \nbillions on WeWork and a dog-walking app just 5  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32320,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "years ago is warning that anyone not using AI will \nbe a goldfish. Having exhausted private-sector  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38400,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "funds, these prophets are now turning to the \npublic sector, pitching AI as a taxpayer-funded  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43520,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "necessity for Western military supremacy.\nThe greatest innovations of the last  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48240,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "century—from the microprocessor to the PC—all \nflourished through organic market demand, without  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53600,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fear-mongering or institutional gaslighting. \nPeople didn’t adopt Wi-Fi, smartphones,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58720,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or GPS under threat of extinction; they adopted \nthem because they worked. Inventors never posed as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65040,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doomsday prophets. Supply trailed demand, products \nevolved through iteration, and mass-market impact  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70720,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was earned—not declared. Today, that logic \nis inverted: billions are poured into scaling  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75520,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a technology that has stagnated and is still \nsearching for its form factor. AI is a narrative  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80640,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "manufactured by tech billionaires and echoed by \npoliticians chasing an economic silver bullet.  \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85920,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yesterday’s crypto and NFT evangelists are today’s \nAI champions.The AI bubble of 2025 is not a carbon  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92960,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "copy of the dot-com bubble, yet the parallels are \nunmistakable. In software: you have unprofitable  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98400,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first-movers with timing but no moat, and a \nswarm of VC-backed startups trying to colonize  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103360,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every vertical with the same platform. In hardware \nthere is a shovel-seller extracting billions on a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108800,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "global dream, pushing infrastructure for demand \nthat hasn’t materialized. And behind them is a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114080,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "presidency that has latched onto this opportunity \nto project American exceptionalism, blunt Asia’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119680,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "manufacturing dominance, and mask economic cracks \nat home. It’s different tech, but the same house  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125280,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of cards—disguised as a free-market boom. \nToday’s LLM wars are a beat-for-beat remake  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131680,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the 1990s browser wars. OpenAI is the new \nNetscape and AOL: first-movers burning cash  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137520,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with no lasting means to capture the value \nthey create. NVIDIA is Sun Microsystems and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142640,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cisco combined. Today’s hyperscalers echo Exodus \nCommunications, spending billions on capacity for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149120,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a revolution with no viable economics. In this \nModern MBA episode, we deconstruct this house  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155440,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of cards that is the AI bubble, peel back layers \nto expose the companies and fragile dependencies  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160800,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "within this tech stack, and reveal why today’s \n$500 billion frenzy is a near-identical  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166160,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "echo of the bubble thirty years ago.\nThe dot-com bubble is significant not just because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171920,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of its similarities to today’s AI bubble, but also \nhow it shaped the tech sector as it exists today.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177840,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The 2000 fallout taught venture capitalists a \nsmarter way to \"pump-and-dump.\" Instead of rushing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182960,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to IPO, VCs now keep startups private, using \nSeries C, D, and E rounds to supercharge growth,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190720,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "control narratives, and inflate valuations through \nbackroom deals within a closed circle of elites,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196000,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "journalists, and fund managers. By the time these \nstartups IPO, their growth curves are spent,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201840,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "future potential is already priced in, and \nthe burden of profitability is dumped onto  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205920,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the public—while VCs and founders exit \nquietly with life-changing fortunes.  \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210960,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today’s AI startups pursue war chests that \nmake the 2010s \"blitzscaling\" look like seed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215600,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rounds. Their media appearances and keynotes are \nchoreographed to sustain hype and VCs behind them  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221200,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "play the same games, evangelizing their portfolio \nand now coordinating IPOs behind-the-scenes so all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227360,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their investments can exit at peak valuations. \nAs a result, the AI bubble is more bloated than  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233280,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "anything in tech history, surpassing the dot-com \nera even when adjusted for inflation. The current  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 239120,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "landscape is as raw as it is saturated. Thousands \nof newcomers have raised millions for a seat at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244640,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the table even as wealth pools around first \nmovers. From B2C wrappers and enterprise agents  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250560,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to model repositories and middleware, every market \nis crowded with look-alike AI startups. Because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256560,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "everyone is \"building the plane while flying \nit,\" differentiation has devolved into a war  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260960,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of benchmarks, where the only moat is a marginally \nbetter column graph. Ultimately, the only way to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266320,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "draw parallels between the AI bubble today and the \ndot-com bubble 25 years ago is to understand the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271760,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entire tech stack—from hardware to software. \nLike Webvan and Pets.com during the dot-com  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277520,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bubble, the highest, frothiest, and most visible \nlayer are the consumer AI startups. They boast the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283200,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most radical value props, generate the greatest \nhype, and burn most of their money on marketing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287840,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just to sustain their astronomical valuations. \nThese startups are \"app-layer\" tenants built on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292960,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the platforms below them; should these underlying \nplatforms raise prices, restrict API access,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298640,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or integrate features natively, then their \nmoats vanish. This fragility extends to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305520,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "various B2B startups in legal, healthcare, \ncoding, and support. For them, the reality is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310800,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same - high burn, no margins, no edge beyond \ntiming, and zero control over the platforms that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317920,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enable their existence. Like Webvan and Pets.com \nin the dot-com bubble, this layer will be the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323840,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first domino to fall. These startups burn record \ncash to outrun not only commoditization but also  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329600,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their own hosts under the belief that their data, \nUI, or brand will be enough to stop customers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336240,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from defecting to cheaper, native, bundled \nalternatives when they inevitably arrive.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340960,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Below the application layer sit the software \ngateways. These are the \"Big Bangs\"—catalysts that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345760,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "drive innovation as much as economic excess. In \nthe 1990s, the web browser was the match that lit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351360,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the fire. Netscape pioneered the graphics-based \nbrowser, transforming the Internet from a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356800,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "text-only academic ghost into an interactive \ndigital frontier with encryption, progressive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361920,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rendering, and JavaScript. Within a year, millions \nflocked to Netscape as the front door to the web.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367840,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Their success invited a swarm of fast-followers, \nignited the dot-com bubble, and culminated in a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372960,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "landmark IPO. As the first mass-market gateway \nto the Internet, Netscape used their user growth,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379440,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "technical achievement, and first-mover advantage \nto justify staggering losses and valuations.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385680,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today, it’s OpenAI who has pioneered the first \nmass-market large language model with ChatGPT as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 391360,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the gateway to generative AI. They justify their \nvaluation and burn with user count and market  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397120,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "timing. Just like in the 90s with Netscape \nand web browsers, LLMs have quickly become a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402000,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "crowded field with many fast-followers who have \nreplicated OpenAI’s technology and products.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407520,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A tech stack is a hierarchy of dependencies, \nwith each layer building on the one beneath  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412240,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it. While software gateways serve millions, they \nremain tenants to the Operating, Distribution,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417760,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Device Platforms. These foundational systems \ncontrol the hardware, OS, and access points. In  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424160,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the browser wars of the 1990s, this layer was \nsimpler. Netscape was tethered to the desktop PC,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429680,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where Microsoft Windows held a monopoly. In \ntoday’s LLM wars of the 2020s, independent LLMs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436240,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "similarly remain at the mercy of multiple hosts. \nChatGPT, Claude, and Perplexity are \"guests\" that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441920,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reach users through the browsers, app stores, \ndevices, and search engines owned by Apple,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 447520,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Microsoft, and Google. They’re unable to penetrate \nthe walled gardens of Big Tech, where the most  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 452240,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "valuable user data and critical tasks reside. \nThis is why Meta has been the most aggressive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457200,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "incumbent in going all-in on AI. To Zuckerberg, \nAI represents a generational opportunity for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463040,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Facebook to finally break its dependency on \nApple and Google. He’s racing to turn LLMs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468320,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into some breakthrough device, OS, and platform \nlike AI-integrated glasses to secure the platform  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471520,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ownership he’s pursued for over a decade.\nBeneath these layers lies the proprietary hardware  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 477360,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enabling software gateways and innovation. In the \n90s, Sun Microsystems and Cisco dominated, selling  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484000,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "high-performance servers and routers essential \nfor dot-com scaling. You could not build an online  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489120,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "business without either. Only Sun servers could \nhandle heavy processing loads; only Cisco switches  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494720,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could route the traffic. B Both enterprises made \nfortunes selling dot-com shovels as companies  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499840,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around the world rushed to scoop up hardware. \nMeanwhile, the U.S. government tightly regulated  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 504880,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exports, classifying Sun servers as national \nsecurity assets and blocking sales to rival  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510320,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "countries under the concern that the compute could \nfuel their military and intelligence advancements.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516480,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "30 years later, the same exact pattern is playing \nout with AI. Today, you can’t build a frontier  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522480,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "LLM without NVIDIA’s GPUs. Because LLMs require \nconstant training for accuracy, every player in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528960,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the stack is trapped in a reckless arms race. They \nmust have access to the most GPUs possible, both  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534560,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in power and quantity, to maintain a competitive \nedge. NVIDIA continues to post record sales  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540160,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quarter after quarter as companies all around \nthe world buy out the latest GPUs before their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545200,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "release. NVIDIA today is Sun and Cisco combined - \nowning the hardware, networking, and programming.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551840,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just like in the 1990s, the U.S. government \nhas placed NVIDIA’s GPUs in strict export  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 557200,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "restrictions to maintain American hegemony.\nBut hardware requires a home. In the 90s, Exodus  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563920,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Communications was the dominant infrastructure \nprovider, hosting the Sun servers and Cisco  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568560,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "routers that powered Google, eBay, and Yahoo. As \nstartups entered the market, they funneled their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574240,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "venture capital to Exodus as rent, which Exodus \nthen used to build even more capacity. When the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580400,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bubble burst in 2001, startups collapsed, their \npayments disappeared, and Exodus went bankrupt  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586080,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with a sprawling empire of unused hardware and \nempty datacenters. Today’s infrastructure layer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591520,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is more accessible but follows the same logic. \nWhile dot-com startups bought and owned their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596960,
    "dDurationMs": 8960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "own hardware, AI companies virtually rent NVIDIA’s \nGPUs on-demand from Amazon AWS, Microsoft Azure,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605920,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Google Cloud. They own, host, and rent out \nmillions of GPUs, CPUs, and disks around the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613760,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "world. These titans have spent decades competing \nto be the ultimate landlords of computing. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620160,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AI frenzy has not only lifted up these big public \ncloud providers, but also birthed neoclouds like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625840,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Coreweave whose entire business model relies \non exclusively hoarding and renting out the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630880,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "highest-end NVIDIA GPUs. What makes this part of \nthe stack fragile is that while dot-com startups  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636480,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stockpiled Sun servers in anticipation for \nfuture growth that never materialized, today’s AI  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 642320,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "startups cling onto unproven “brute-force” scaling \nlaws that more GPUs and more data yields more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648960,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "intelligence. But unlike the dot-com startups, \nthey’re not renting to scale - they’re renting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 656480,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to survive. It’s a dangerous cycle: startups burn \nventure capital to rent GPUs, cloud providers use  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664560,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that cash to buy more chips from NVIDIA and build \nmore datacenters. It’s a ridiculous loop sustained  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670240,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solely by the speculation that infinite scaling \nwill eventually yield a profitable product.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675840,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At the base of our stack is utility. In the 90s, \nthe bottleneck was bandwidth. While Sun provided  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 681360,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the server-side power, it couldn't fix the \nclient-side; users surfing the web faced constant  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686720,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "congestion, timeouts, and sluggish loading. \nLegacy telephone copper lines could not handle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 691760,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the millions of users and massive data packets \nthat the emerging web demanded. This physical  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697200,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "constraint sparked fears that the dot-com industry \nwould collapse, triggering an arms race within  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701920,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the private sector to build the digital railroad \nfor the 21st century. The promise of high-margin,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707760,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "recurring revenue pushed telecom companies \nto spend trillions and borrow billions to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713120,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lay millions of miles of fiber. However, when the \ndot-com bubble burst, giants like WorldCom and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 718800,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Global Crossing collapsed, having over-leveraged \nthemselves to build for a future that had not yet  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724080,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "arrived. Today’s bottleneck for AI is energy. \nModern chips are far more power-intensive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729680,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than 90s servers. Public grids can’t supply \nelectricity to run and cool NVIDIA’s GPUs at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736160,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the pace the industry is demanding as a single \nAI-focused data center can consume as much power  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740960,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as 100,000 households. With public grids unable \nto scale, Big Tech is aggressively investing in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747360,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nuclear energy to hoard the electricity \nrequired for their AI workloads.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752480,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Companies are obsessed with AI for its promise \nto lower costs and it’s only made it harder to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757040,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "speak to a real human. Corporations would rather \nconnect you with AI chatbots whose only purpose  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760720,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is to waste your time and save them money. \nEveryone is building AI for shareholders and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765440,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no one is building AI for the consumer.  \nAt Modern MBA, we’re the biggest AI critics.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770480,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When Kudos approached us saying their AI could \nlower our Internet and phone bills without us  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 775040,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "having to pick up the phone or write a single \nword, it sounded like Silicon Valley vaporware.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779440,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then we tried it ourselves. Kudos successfully \nnegotiated down our Xfinity bill down by 22% and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784880,
    "dDurationMs": 44480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "increased our download speeds by 10%.\nKudos is AI that fights for the person  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 829360,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "paying the bill rather than the companies \ncollecting it. No more hours wasted on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 833280,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hold. No more grumpy agents. Just money and \ntime saved on your biggest expenses. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 838000,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Kudos takes the fight to all the service providers \nand credit card companies who have buried  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842240,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "consumers with legalese, deceptive UI, policy \ntraps, and offshore call centers. For decades,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 848240,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these corporations have made it intentionally \nhard to talk to a real human so that customers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852800,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "give up trying to cancel or negotiate their \nbills. That all changes with Kudos.  \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857280,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Kudos’s AI voice agent is so natural that \nnot even your T-Mobile, Verizon, Comcast, and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 862080,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "DirectTV rep will know it’s AI. The results speak \nfor themselves. The average Kudos user saves over  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 867920,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "$250 per provider per year. There’s no contest \n- RocketMoney charges you $250 per year, takes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 874560,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "50% of every dollar saved, and drags negotiations \nout for nearly 2 weeks. In comparison, Kudos saves  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 880400,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you the same amount in 10 minutes - and you keep \n100% of all those savings in your pocket.  \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 885440,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Negotiating your bill is just the beginning. \nKudos’s voice agent will soon help you dispute  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 890160,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "charges, secure the best retention offer - and \nyou’ll never waste a single second on hold.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895280,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s not just AI. Kudos also cancels unwanted \nsubscriptions in seconds, maximizes benefits on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 901360,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every purchase by picking the best card from \nyour wallet, stacks rewards you didn’t even  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 905760,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "know existed, and offers cash back at over 15,000 \nwebsites at rates even higher than Rakuten. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 911520,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Download Kudos today and let AI fight for you, not \nthe corporations. Kudos is available for free on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 917680,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "iOS, Android, and in your favorite browser as \nan extension. Modern MBA viewers get a $20 cash  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 922960,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bonus after their first confirmed purchase on \na Kudos partner website with the referral code  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 927520,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ModernMBA. Thank you to Kudos for fighting for \nconsumers and making this episode possible.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 933280,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The money flows from the top \ndown. When venture capital,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 936480,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "speculation, and demand dry up at \nthe application and gateway layers,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940720,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the entire house of cards collapses. Ultimately, \nOpenAI is not a 1:1 for Netscape, nor NVIDIA for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 948480,
    "dDurationMs": 960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sun. The tech stack today is fragmented with \nmultiple incumbents, not just one giant. Unlike  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 949440,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their predecessors of Netscape, AOL, Yahoo, \nand Sun, who tunnel-visioned on a single layer,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 953440,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "today’s tech giants are diversified \"walled \ngardens\" with ownership across the entire stack,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 958080,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from OS and browsers to the hardware itself. The \nexecutives, boardrooms, and venture capitalists  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 964000,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leading Big Tech are 90s tech workers forged in \nthe dot-com fallout. They are all ultra-sensitive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 970960,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to being \"abstracted away”, maintaining armies \nof engineers to institutionalize emerging tech,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 979520,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "account teams to lock-in Fortune 500 clients, \nand M\u0026A divisions ready to absorb promising  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 986080,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "startups before they become threats. And no CEO \nwants to be the one who screwed the pooch like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 993760,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Marissa Mayer at Yahoo, Carly Fiorina at HP, Steve \nBallmer at Microsoft, or John Schulley at Apple. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1001040,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is why Meta, Google, Microsoft, and Apple \nare all forcing AI into every product and service  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1006800,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "without a clear user need or endgame. They would \nrather subsidize adoption and accelerate this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1011760,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "uncertain future toward its own commoditization \nthan cede an inch to a newcomer. With their walled  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1016960,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gardens and margins, these giants can afford \nto burn billions. In the worst-case scenario,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1022000,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AI will just be another write-off for Big Tech - \nno different than the Apple Car, the Metaverse,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1027120,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Windows Phone, or Google’s various failed \nexperiments. Their collective spending spree  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1032400,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is an insurance policy against a total platform \nshift. If LLMs become the primary touchpoint,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1038240,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a new, AI-native hardware device could \nsupplant the iPhone. If people stop Googling,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1042800,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alphabet loses its search monopoly. If AI agents \ntake over, the need for companies to buy Microsoft  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1048800,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Office disappears as the underlying Excel, Docs, \nand Powerpoints become invisible. If AI becomes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1056880,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the primary way humans interact and work, the \nbillion-dollar interfaces that make Big Tech \"Big\"  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1063440,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "risk becoming low-value, invisible components. \nJust as web browsers eventually became free,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1069280,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "integrated offerings, Big Tech is betting \nthat LLMs will follow suit as commodity  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1074560,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "add-ons rather than independent, \nstandalone, proprietary services.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1079040,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ultimately, it is a matter of when, not if, \nthe dominoes fall and the sequence is clear.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1084160,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Every participant has actively inflated this \nbubble for personal enrichment but it’s OpenAI  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1089600,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that warrants the most scrutiny. Sam Altman is \nthe architect of the AI bubble, having inflated  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1095040,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "software innovation into a trillion-dollar global \nnarrative with capital raises and promises that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1100080,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dwarf any in Silicon Valley history. \nHis rhetoric has emboldened hyperbole,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1104590,
    "dDurationMs": 3410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shielded the private sector from layoff scrutiny, \nsecured sector-wide immunity for IP theft, and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1108000,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gifted the political class a convenient diversion \nfrom domestic woes. This trillion-dollar house of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1113840,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cards has been forged through backroom deals, \npolitical favoritism, and circular financing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1118720,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and has bound every company in the stack to the \nsame fate. Beyond Altman’s personal motives,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1124880,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OpenAI is at a breaking point. Breakthrough \ninnovation is no guarantee of economic success,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1129840,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and a massive user base is meaningless without \na path to profitability. OpenAI’s p osition is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1135360,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "both enviable and untenable—a mirror of \nNetscape in the 1990s: a market-making,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1141120,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "category-defining pioneer destined to be swallowed \nby the underlying platforms that it depends on.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1146960,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Netscape was the ChatGPT of the dot-com era. It \nrevolutionized internet interaction, ignited the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1153280,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "browser wars, and leveraged a freemium model to \ncommand a 90% market share. But while innovation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1158960,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fueled its meteoric rise and historic IPO, it \ncouldn’t pay the bills. Netscape eventually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1164160,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "died because it failed to build a viable business \nbefore the walls closed in. It was Microsoft who  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1169440,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "delivered the fatal blow. Bill Gates realized the \nweb was more engaging than the Windows desktop;  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1174960,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "he feared that the more time users spent \nin a third-party browser, the less they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1179040,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would value the OS. If developers prioritized \nNetscape over Windows, his empire was dead.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1186400,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Microsoft retaliated by bundling Internet Explorer \nas the pre-installed default in Windows and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1191040,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "forcing OEMs to exclude Netscape. While Netscape \nretained its user base, it was effectively choked  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1196960,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "off from growth—unable to compete with native \ndistribution without exhausting capital.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1203280,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before its collapse, Netscape tried for years \nto transition from a breakthrough product into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1208000,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a sustainable business. It adopted a \"freemium\" \nmodel, though it rarely enforced its $50 licenses  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1213840,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out of fear of slowing user growth. It tried to \nbecome an enterprise \"super-browser\"—a unified  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1219280,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "B2B interface for email and reports across all \noperating systems. But Microsoft and IBM quickly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1225040,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "boxed Netscape out by bundling similar services \nfor free into their own software suites. By 1998,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1231680,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a desperate Netscape pivoted back to \nconsumer advertising, but it was too late.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1237120,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Internet Explorer had cannibalized its market \nshare as the \"built-in\" alternative, while Yahoo  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1242000,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and AOL had already secured dominance upstream \nas the web’s primary ad hubs. Facing bankruptcy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1248160,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Netscape sold itself to AOL—another independent \ngateway seeking platform ownership. Ironically,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1254400,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the web browser that set the dot-com bubble \nin motion did not live to see its peak. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1259360,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AOL was eventually crushed by the same forces \nthat swallowed Netscape. Its business was built  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1264640,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entirely on legacy telephone lines. Once cable \ncompanies built high-speed broadband, AOL’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1270480,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dial-up subscribers evaporated and so did their ad \nbusiness as users abandoned its curated portal for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1276320,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the freedom of the open web and Google Search.\nThe parallels extend into politics. Just as the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1284000,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Trump administration has anchored its strategy \nto AI, the Clinton administration architected the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1289280,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"Information Superhighway\". Through the 1991 High \nPerformance Computing and 1996 Telecommunications  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1295280,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Act, the federal government provided legal \nprotection, waived regulations for dot-com  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1299920,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "companies, and mandated Netscape for millions \nof students and employees. The administration  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1307840,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "advocated Netscape, Cisco switches, and \nSun servers abroad so that the U.S. could  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1313200,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dominate the global web. And when Microsoft began \nbundling Internet Explorer for free into Windows,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1318960,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the DOJ launched an antitrust lawsuit as a \ntaxpayer-funded attempt to protect Netscape.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1324320,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "30 later, the U.S. government is running the \nsame play. The AI Litigation Task Force and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1330960,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Genesis Mission grant OpenAI legal immunity \nfrom copyright liability, IP infringement,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1336480,
    "dDurationMs": 8880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and responsibility from AI-generated output. \nThrough the CHIPS Act, NVIDIA has effectively  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1345360,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "become a branch of American foreign policy. Just \nlike how the Clinton administration brokered  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1350960,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "international deals for Sun and Cisco, the \ncurrent administration is doing the same and using  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1356000,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "taxpayer funds to buy GPUs in the name of public \ninterest - all which further enrich NVIDIA.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1362960,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AOL and Netscape were first-movers that defined \nthe mass market but failed as businesses. They  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1368320,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were independent gateways where users merely \npassed through. The $60 billion Sam Altman has  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1373920,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "raised merely buys him time to find a moat. \nThe company’s latest moves and internal panic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1378720,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over Google Gemini 3 says everything. Apple, \nGoogle, and Microsoft are using the Internet  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1383840,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Explorer playbook to suffocate OpenAI through \nattrition. When Apple Intelligence on an iPhone,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1390320,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Copilot in Excel, or Gemini in Google Docs \nis \"good enough,\" the convenience of a free  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1396960,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "default will always defeat performance and first \nmover advantage. And while titans can indefinitely  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1402960,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "absorb massive losses, OpenAI must somehow defend \ntheir subscriber base without running out of money  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1408720,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "themselves. They have no leverage - if they \nraise prices or fall behind on performance,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1416000,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ChatGPT customers will simply churn toward the \nnearest integrated alternative. Any delay in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1420720,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "GPT-6 or GPT-7 will allow incumbents to \nclose the gap. In this self-made bubble,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1426160,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OpenAI cannot afford silence; it must ship \nconstantly—even if only for optics—to avoid the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1432240,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perception of stagnation. From this perspective, \n\"agentic AI\" is less a technical epiphany and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1438000,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more a calculated attempt to puncture Big \nTech’s walled gardens without triggering an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1442560,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all-out war. By using agents to book flights, \nmanage calendars, and order food through what  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1448720,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "appears on a user's screen, OpenAI can bypass \nAPI restrictions, platform policies, and harvest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1456320,
    "dDurationMs": 9360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "data without violating Terms of Service.  \nThey’re throwing everything at the wall to find  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1465680,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a moat before their window slams shut. They spent \nover $6 billion to bring on Jony Ive in the hopes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1471360,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of designing AI-native devices that can disrupt \nthe smartphone. They’ve launched a browser,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1475360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "embedded text-to-video generation, created their \nown app store, and released SearchGPT Shopping to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1480560,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "facilitate online transactions. Their ultimate bet \nis in physical infrastructure. The taxpayer-funded  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1486480,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "$500 billion Stargate would be the world’s most \npowerful supercomputer. The hope is to create a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1491680,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "computing monopoly so strong that Big Tech will \nbe forced to rent their hardware to keep up. It’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1498000,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only a matter of time before OpenAI will be forced \nto monetize its gateway directly, where ChatGPT  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1503600,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "responses will be mixed with ads and user data \nwill be auctioned off to the highest bidder. This  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1508720,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "situation is not unique to OpenAI. In this layer, \nbetter doesn’t mean survival. In the dot-com era,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1512240,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Opera was the underdog browser - pioneering speed, \nsize, tabs, session recovery, and pop-up blocking.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1517760,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But despite leading on innovation, ad revenue \nwas never enough and Opera was still eventually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1522640,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "obliterated by Internet Explorer. In this \nAI bubble, as in the 90s, convenience and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1528240,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distribution are the only moats that matter. \nAnthropic faces the same issues as OpenAI.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1534160,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just as Netscape discovered, OpenAI is realizing \nthe hard way that general-purpose gateways grow  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1539840,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the fastest but are the first to be suffocated by \ncommoditization. They are the hardest to defend  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1544720,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and sustain as independent businesses. \nYet Netscape was ultimately not wrong.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1550640,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They viewed the Internet as a paradigm shift \nthat would democratize commerce and launch a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1554880,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "software revolution—all of which have come true \n20 years later. Netscape wasn't the right company  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1561840,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nor did it survive long enough to realize the \nfuture it founded. Sun envisioned a world where  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1568000,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"the network is the computer,\" where data would \nreside online and that distributed power would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1572400,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be sold like electricity from a grid. Today, \nwe live that reality: accessing data across  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1577840,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "multiple devices and renting compute from the \ncloud. Cisco prophesied a convergence of voice,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1582960,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "video, and data that would liberate workers from \ncubicles and desktops. All these pioneers were  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1589840,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "right about what would happen, but they \ncould not have known how. Ultimately,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1594720,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it took Apple to provide the missing piece—the \nsmartphone—to turn these theories into reality.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1601280,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OpenAI may be right about LLMs being the pathway \nto AGI, how they’ll transform industries, unlock  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1607440,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "greater productivity, and redefine human purpose. \nBut like the pioneers before them, they’re  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1612560,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trapped in a bubble of their own making. The AI \nrevolution, much like the Internet's, will take  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1619680,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decades and require further breakthroughs that \nno single startup can achieve. The hard truth is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1625280,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that this bubble is not necessary. But by framing \nAI as an existential, techno-theological crisis,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1631600,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OpenAI - and all the other startups following \nthis narrative - have effectively socialized  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1636240,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the risks while privatizing the speculative gains \nfor themselves. When this bubble finally bursts,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1643040,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the public will bear the wreckage, while the \ncircle of tech billionaires and political  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1647200,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "elites profit - free to manufacture the next \nbubble to fuel their fortunes and power.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1654160,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Every business is asking the same question: how \ndo we make AI work for us? The possibilities are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1659440,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "endless, and guessing is too risky. Here’s the \nproblem: while you’re trying to figure it out,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1664080,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the competition is already moving. And \nevery day you sit on the sidelines,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1668080,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you’re losing ground. Forget the buzzwords \nand 'snake oil' pitches. With NetSuite,  "
    } ]
  }, {
